// apply_ability_smoke — task #138 smoke fixture for the registry-driven
// dispatch path (#136 / slice γ). The physics rule reads each agent's
// `level` SoA field (u32) and feeds it into `apply_ability` as the
// AbilityId — the runtime would register an AbilityProgram for that
// id and upload a PackedAbilityRegistry; the WGSL kernel walks the
// SoA columns and writes chronicle records into the shared `event_ring`.
//
// `level` is a stand-in: the DSL doesn't support custom user-declared
// SoA fields today (only the predefined columns in `EntityFieldCatalog`
// lower), so any u32 SoA field works for the smoke fixture's purpose.
// The runtime would write a real ability id into `level` (or a future
// dedicated field) when this codepath finally goes live.
//
// **Dead at runtime today** — no runtime crate references this fixture.
// Its purpose is to drive the full `dsl_compiler` pipeline
// (parse → resolve → lower → schedule → emit) so any regression in
// the apply_ability codepath surfaces as a CI failure rather than as a
// silent breakage of the dispatcher arms once a runtime wires it up.
//
// **Closed-loop fixture (2026-05-06):** the closed-loop demo lives
// in `apply_ability_chronicle_consumer.sim` (and
// `apply_ability_chronicle_consumer_runtime`). The dispatcher writes
// `EffectDamageApplied = 26`; the consumer's PerEvent rule reads
// kind=26 directly because engine event names are aliased to their
// hardcoded discriminants at resolve time
// (`dsl_ast::engine_events::engine_event_kind_id_for_name`). The
// fixture trips P6 on `agents.set_hp` from a PerEvent rule, mirroring
// `duel_abilities.sim::ApplyDamage`, and is tolerated the same way.
//
// **#138 entry point.** The next substantive slice — wiring one
// `duel_abilities.sim` verb (e.g. Strike) through apply_ability instead
// of the verb→emit→ApplyDamage cascade pattern — picks up here:
//   1. Choose a duel verb (Strike is simplest: single Damage effect).
//   2. Define the verb's AbilityProgram in a runtime crate
//      (`AbilityProgram::new_single_target` with `EffectOp::Damage`).
//   3. Upload it via PackedAbilityRegistry (#135 already lands the
//      buffer-upload helper).
//   4. Replace the verb's `emit Damaged{...}` with `apply_ability <id>
//      by self target target` — operand types match the slice-ε type
//      check (caster/target → CgTy::AgentId).
//   5. Parity assert: chronicle records from the new path equal the
//      records the existing `ApplyDamage` physics rule writes for the
//      same (caster, target, tick, world_seed) tuple.
// The slice-ε type check (well_formed.rs) ensures step 4's operand
// expressions are u32-compatible at lowering — see
// apply_ability_rejects_f32_target_at_lowering for the regression pin.
//
// What this exercises that the unit tests in `wgsl_body.rs` don't:
//   - the AST/parser surface (`apply_ability <expr>`),
//   - lowering (`IrStmt::ApplyAbility` → `CgStmt::ApplyAbility`),
//   - the BGL composer's recording of `EventRing(Append)` writes on
//     ApplyAbility-bearing ops (added in commit `1779b0e6`),
//   - the dispatcher's full integration with the surrounding kernel
//     (binding-set composition, cfg uniform, `agent_id` preamble),
//   - **slice ε** (#161 + d0bc37fd): the explicit `by <caster>` and
//     `target <target>` clauses — the second rule below pins the
//     surface in the .sim corpus (not just inline-source tests).
//
// Mirror sim file shape: `target_chaser.sim` — minimum viable
// (event Tick + entity Agent + one PerAgent physics rule).

event Tick { }

entity Hero : Agent { }

physics DispatchAbility @phase(per_agent) {
  on Tick {} where (self.alive) {
    apply_ability agents.level(self)
  }
}

// Slice ε surface pin: same shape as DispatchAbility but with the
// explicit `by self target self` clauses. Lowering populates the
// caster + target operands from the resolved expressions; the
// dispatcher's chronicle writes route through `caster_slot` and
// `target_slot` (both lower to `agent_id` here — equivalent to the
// implicit defaults but proves the parser/lowering/emit path for
// the explicit-operand surface).
//
// When the DSL grows custom-target syntax (e.g. `target
// query.nearest_hostile_to(self, range)`), this rule is the natural
// place to upgrade — the inline-source tests in
// `tests/apply_ability_smoke.rs` exercise corner cases; this is the
// "boring real sim that uses the production-shape syntax" pin.
physics DispatchAbilityExplicit @phase(per_agent) {
  on Tick {} where (self.alive) {
    apply_ability agents.level(self) by self target self
  }
}
